{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>I struck</b> — I already mentioned in the book of Amos that the blight-stroke is on standing grain, as in: <i>shadufot qadim</i> ( blasted by the east wind)." "<b>And the mildew</b> — [it means] the green-rot, and the proof that it is so is that he mentioned the hail." "<b>And the meaning of 'all the work of your hands'</b> — because the smiting of HaShem encompassed everything, the opposite of: 'and HaShem your Elohim will bless you in all the work of your hands which you do' (Deuteronomy 14:29, 16:15)." "<b>['Yet you did not turn to Me']</b> — the meaning is: you did not return to Me. And now it is clear that HaShem disciplined them — perhaps they would return from their evil way — but they did not return; therefore He gave them the aforementioned parable."], :book :haggai, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra cross-references his own commentary on Amos 4:9, where the same agricultural term appears; the quoted phrase is from Genesis 41:6, blighted by the east wind, shown in Hebrew per protocol.", 1 "The logic is compressed: since hail is listed alongside, the reference is to agricultural damage as in the plagues motif, confirming the reading of ירקון as a fungal blight.", 2 "I read מאירת as a form of מכה based on the parallelism of striking versus blessing established immediately after."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Haggai" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/hagai"] ["Ibn Ezra on Haggai -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/hagai"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:49:38.441728394Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}